Z||000011||ChadMan||09-14-2000||09:33 AM||||I always wanted to make a naked Fiero into a dune buggy.  Remove all body panels and mount some KC HiLites on the top of the naked frame.  Start with an 84 with 4.10, so it has plenty of low-end grunt.  Note that with the wheelwells gone, there's plenty of room to mount 15x6 steel wheels with off-road tires.  The approach and departure angles are pretty good with the body panels gone, so it could get in and out of some rough stuff.  I bet it would be the offroading equal of a Baja Bug and would probably do better than the wimpier SUV's like the CR-V and RAV4.  You'd have to figure a way to keep the ECM dry.<P>What do you guys think?||||reg||3
Z||000012||alpha||09-14-2000||12:45 PM||||I thought I would post of my parts car...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Speeking of Fiero Buggies, have you seen the Jalapeno from cybercars? It looks pretty sweet. I don't think it would be first on my list of kit cars to build tho.<P>&lt;IMG SRC=http://130.111.89.156/dsc_081.jpg&gt;<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||ChadMan||09-14-2000||01:00 PM||||The Jalapeno is cool, alpha, but I was trying to think of something you could do really cheap.  I mean, $800 or so for an 84 that runs good and looks crappy, minus all the body and interior parts you could sell...you might be able to build this thing for free!<P>And after I spent all that money on the Jalapeno's body, I'd be afraid to take it off-road where it might get scratched or cracked.  My Fiero-buggy, hell, you could take it anywhere!  With 15" wheels and 75-series tires, it ought to have at least 8" of ground clearance - better than most SUV's.<P>Sometimes I'm such a redneck.  Oh well - gotta buy a house first...||||reg||1
